OAuth 401 gateway cleanup diff
==============================

Base
----
Feature branch: codex/oauth-401-auto-cleanup
Original baseline: 119540e8404d5e9661370a2b18854bb0ee1fb911
Original gateway behavior: OAuth HTTP 401 invalidated the token cache and used the existing cooldown/error path.

Setting and API
---------------
- Added persisted setting oauth_401_cleanup_settings with field enabled=false by default.
- Added GET /api/v1/admin/settings/oauth-401-cleanup.
- Added PUT /api/v1/admin/settings/oauth-401-cleanup with payload {"enabled":true|false}.
- GET requires the existing settings read scope; PUT requires settings write/settings.manage.
- Added a destructive-action card under Gateway Service -> Gateway Resilience with confirmation and Chinese/English messages.

Gateway branch
--------------
- HandleUpstreamError now checks the enabled policy for an upstream HTTP 401.
- Grok's separate upstream error handler applies the same policy.
- Only a direct account with type=oauth can enter the deletion repository.
- API Key, Setup Token, and credential-shadow accounts retain their previous handling.
- The setting read is fail-closed and cached for five seconds with atomic storage and singleflight.
- A CAS miss means concurrent reauthorization won; the account is preserved, the stale request fails over, and no account-level runtime block is added.
- A deletion error preserves the account and falls back to the existing 401 cooldown/error behavior.

Transactional deletion
----------------------
- Locks the credential owner and compares the complete credentials JSONB document, including _token_version.
- Soft-deletes the owner and linked shadows in one PostgreSQL transaction.
- Removes account-group bindings, IPv6 egress bindings, and scheduled test plans.
- Writes one scheduler account-changed outbox event per deleted account.
- Removes scheduler snapshots, invalidates token/session/stat caches, and installs a short deleted-account block against concurrent stale snapshots.

